lesson g6-volume-of-prisms-and-cylinders v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=5 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Volume of prisms and cylinders"
  goal "Find the volume of a prism or a cylinder as the area of its base times its height, and give it in litres."

  look {
    scene 36x12 {
      cubestack stack l=4 w=3 h=3 count=1 at=canvas(1, 1)
      solid silo kind=cylinder label="" right-of=stack gap=2
      solid roof kind=prism label="" right-of=silo gap=2
    }
    say """
      A box of cubes is layers: 12 cubes in a layer, three layers, 36 cubes. Every prism works the
      same way. Its end is the base, and the prism is that base stacked up along its height or its
      length. So the volume is the area of the base times the height, for a cuboid, for the
      triangle-ended roof of the mill, and for the round grain cylinder, whose base is a circle of
      area radius times radius times 3.14. A litre is 1000 cm³.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice volume.mill-sack-layers count=1 seed=3
    level easy {
      worked volume.grain-cylinder r=3 h=2 level=medium
    }
    practice volume.mill-roof-prism count=1 seed=4
    practice volume.grain-cylinder count=1 seed=5
    practice volume.tank-litres count=1 seed=6
    practice volume.wider-grain-cylinder count=1 seed=2
    show volume.cylinder-tank-height r=4 h=2 easy-r=1 easy-h=3 hard-r=5 hard-h=4
    level hard {
      practice volume.tank-litres count=1 seed=11
    }
    practice volume.mill-roof-prism count=1 seed=8
    practice volume.grain-cylinder count=1 seed=9 easy-seed=13 hard-seed=14
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show volume.diameter-for-radius d=4 h=5 easy-d=6 easy-h=10 hard-d=8 hard-h=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show volume.roll-the-sheet s=1 easy-s=0 hard-s=2
  }
  remember {
    say "The volume of a prism or a cylinder is the area of its base times its height. A litre is 1000 cm³."
  }

  grown-ups """
    One rule does every solid here: find the area of the end, then multiply by how far it goes. Ask
    the child to name the base first, since a triangular prism lying down has its base standing up
    at the end. The common slips are forgetting to halve for the triangle, using the distance round
    a circle instead of its area, and using the diameter as the radius. For litres, 1000 cm³ make a
    litre and 1000 litres a cubic metre. In the second reasoning question a cylinder twice as wide
    holds four times as much, because the circle grows in both directions. In the two-star question
    Leo's answer is four times too big for the same reason. In the three-star question the fat roll
    holds more, by exactly the ratio of the sides, and a child who tries numbers has done real work.
    If area of the base is still shaky, stay here another day with cuboids. Off the page, fill a tin
    with water from a measuring jug and check its volume against the formula.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the boxes are at most 5 by 3 by 4, a cylinder of radius 3 m and height 2 m is worked in the teacher's pen, the roofs are 4 or 6 m wide and 8 or 10 m long, the cylinders have radius 1 or 3 m, the tanks are in centimetres, the wider cylinder is twice as wide and as tall, the tank's height is found for a base of radius 1 m, Leo's tank is 6 m across, and the sheet is 20 by 10 cm."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the boxes are 6 long and up to 6 layers, the roofs are 8 m wide and 12 or 15 m long, the cylinders have radius 5 or 6 m, the tanks are in metres and asked twice, apart, the wider cylinder is three times as wide and twice as tall, Leo's tank is 8 m across, and the sheet is 30 by 10 cm."
  }
}
